{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments ["<b>These are the generations of Noah — Noah was a righteous man, etc.</b> Rashi explained: since Scripture mentioned him, it narrates his praise. If so, it should have narrated his praise at the end of Genesis (5:32), for there too it mentioned him. Rather, because it is stated here ״אֵלֶּה תּוֹלְדוֹת״, and every place where ״אֵלֶּה״ is said invalidates the preceding ones — to tell you that the generations of all the earlier ones are not called generations, for their memory will be blotted out in the Flood and they were as if they had never been, but these are called enduring generations — therefore here it was necessary to state a good reason why these survived more than the rest: because ״נֹחַ אִישׁ צַדִּיק תָּמִים״ etc.; therefore all his generations went to eternal life, and all the earlier generations to reproach and everlasting disgrace. And according to another matter that Rashi explained — to teach you that the principal generations of the righteous are good deeds — he mentioned ״אֵלֶּה״ to exclude mere physical progeny, to tell you: these are his principal generations. And this is recalled here because HaShem closed his womb, so to speak, and he did not beget many sons and daughters, and perhaps Noah would grieve over this. Therefore it says that besides this he had many generations of good deeds — better than sons and daughters." "<b>A righteous man, whole-hearted he was in his generations.</b> Because the generation of the Flood corrupted themselves, degenerating in three kinds of corruption mentioned in the parashah — namely idolatry, forbidden sexual relations, and robbery — therefore it prefaced by saying that Noah was fenced against all three. For ״אִישׁ צַדִּיק״ — this means that he did not rob from what belonged to others. ״תָּמִים הָיָה בְּדֹרֹתָיו״ — this means that he was fenced against forbidden sexual relations, for he was born circumcised and was whole. ״אֶת הָאֱלֹהִים הִתְהַלֶּךְ נֹחַ״ — he did not turn to other elohim. But the generation of the Flood breached the fence of all three."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 6, :notes {0 "The phrase כָּבַשׁ ה׳ מַעְיָינוֹ, literally 'HaShem conquered his spring,' is rendered idiomatically as 'closed his womb, so to speak.'"}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:27:16.507040490Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}